  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a strong ridge of high pressure anchored \nover the southwest US with a mid-level trough centered to the\nnorth of Lake Superior. At the surface, a cold front associated\nwith the aforementioned trough stretches from northern \nWisconsin southward into central Iowa, with convection ongoing\nahead of the boundary in western Wisconsin as of 2 am. A little\ncloser to home, warm air advection out ahead of the front has \nled to widespread mid-level stratus, which is holding \ntemperatures higher by several degrees compared to this time 24 \nhours ago. Some CAMs suggest spotty, light showers may develop \nwithin this region through this morning (15% chance), but most \nshould stay dry. \n\nThe mid-level shortwave will track through the Northern Great Lakes \nRegion today, sending a cold front through Illinois this afternoon \nand evening. Out ahead of the front, temperatures are forecast to \nwarm into the 80s paired with low 70s dewpoints, resulting in \nmoderate destabilization by this afternoon. SBCAPE values look \nto peak around 2500 J/kg combined with bulk shear around 30 kts,\nwhich would be enough to support a few organized storms. CAMs \nshow scattered storms developing near or east of I-55 by late \nthis afternoon, then spreading east through the evening. The \nprimary hazards with any storms that do become organized will be\ndamaging wind gusts and isolated large hail. The threat for \nflash flooding appears low, though pockets of heavy rain will be\npossible under any of the stronger storms.\n\nNorthwest flow aloft remains in place through late week with a broad \narea of surface high pressure controlling the weather pattern \nthrough at least Saturday. This will keep things dry and\ncomfortable with seasonal temperatures in the low to middle \n80s. Upper ridging will begin shifting east into the Midwest \nstates this weekend, bringing the return of summer-like heat as \nsurface flow becomes southerly. Temperatures will climb into the\nupper 80s to low 90s beginning Sunday, with more tropical-like \ndewpoints sending heat indices into the middle to upper 90s \nby Monday. A couple upper shortwaves riding the northern \nperiphery of the ridge could bring showers and storms back into \nthe area Monday and Tuesday, though most of this should remain \nconfined over the northern half of the state.",
